// 1. Why to learn TypeScript
/*
There is a lot of hurry in the market to jump into TypeScript and don't even learn JavaScript. But that is not how it should be started.
It should be started with JS.

Everybody knows one thing about TypeScript: TS is a superset of JavaScript — everything you can do in JS is available in TS and a lot more. But it's not adding more features, it just allows you to write JS in a much precise manner, so that your code faces a lot less error in the runtime. If there is any error, it is already being displayed to you while writing the code. In fact, all the code that you write in TS is finally compiled into JS.

TypeScript is not about reinventing the entire JavaScript language; it's just asking that you write JS with a little but more accurate behavior, in a more precise manner, so that are less errors. If you're using TS, you need to use the super power of TS to make your code much more powerful and much more error prone. TS is all about Type Safety, nothing more.

JavaScript allows us to do some odd behavior. Examples:
2 + '2' = '22'
null + 2 = 2
undefined + 2 = NaN
Shouldn't be allowed to do (Type Safety was missing). All not the quirky behavior of JS, but rather you haven't been concerned about types.

The ideia is: I shouldn't be reading that much [the language specification given by ECMAScript] on the first place; I should be focused on building my application with Type Safety. And that is where the TypeScript comes into the picture.
*/

// 2. TypeScript is not what you think
/*
The simple idea behind TypeScript is static checking. That's the only job of TS. Static checking is present in many languages, such as Java or Golang. A lot of languages are built in with the static checking and it means that whenever you're writing the code, the parser of the language or the entire syntax of the language is constantly being analyzed by the IDE's. It's not the case of JavaScript — only when the code is executed the errors appear. It would be really a great help if there is a static checker which checks my code as I try to write it. TypeScript is just for static checking. It just analyze your code and help you to understand that you might be running into a potential error without even running the code and getting the errors at the runtime itself. That's all it does. The whole job of TS is simply to analyze the code.

It is still possible that your code might be giving you squiggly lines or errors and still you may export this entire code into JavaScript and that might run absolutely well.

In TypeScript, you write a lot more code compared to JavaScript. But it is worth it.

How the development process works: you write your code in .ts format — when you're building components like React or something, I have seen people use a simple extension of .tsx, which is also totally fine (it is just a mixture of TypeScript plus the gsx syntax) — and that code is transpiled into JavaScript. And there are a lot of fancy words here: it is compiled, transpiled... We'll work through with that. You can just simply say at this point of time that TS code is being converted into JS.

And one thing, the most important thing to learn and to know: TypeScript is a development tool. Your project still runs in JavaScript. The code that you produce or you send to the production is still a pure 100% JavaScript code. So TS is just a layer on top of JS (superset). It helps you to write better code — code with less problems (chance of errors), more scalable and maintainable, and easily understandable. TS is a development tool, a wrapper around JS to make your life easier and safer.

https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/
*/

